<section class="block">
<h2>An old idea, newly practical</h2>
<p>"Lights out" manufacturing is not new. The idea of factories that run unattended has been around for decades, and the term "dark factory" comes from plants that can operate with the lights off because no one needs to see<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. What is new is how fast the enabling technology is spreading. The International Federation of Robotics reported in September 2026 that China installed about 354,000 industrial robots in 2025, around 59% of new installations worldwide<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p>Even so, fully dark plants remain rare. In a September 2026 report on car manufacturing, experts said they knew of no fully lights-out mass vehicle plant, though some plants have sections where people are hardly seen<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The reality is a spectrum: more of each line runs unattended, while people handle changeovers, maintenance, exceptions and quality decisions.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The same idea, applied to software</h2>
<p>In 2026 the term crossed into software. A "dark software factory" is a pipeline where AI agents plan, write, test and ship code with little or no human reading of it. The most discussed example is a security company's small team that described a factory where humans write specifications and watch scores while agents do the rest, a model the Stanford CodeX blog questioned on the grounds of who can trust agent-validated testing<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>.</p>
<p>The more careful voices draw a lesson from manufacturing. OpenHands argues that the history matters more than the metaphor, and that the approach works best for well-defined work where correctness can be checked automatically<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Addy Osmani describes a spectrum between "light" and "dark" factories, rather than a switch<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. That matches the factory floor: most of the value comes long before the last person leaves.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Fully dark</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>No one watching the line<em>rare in factories and software</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Mostly unattended</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">People handle exceptions<em>where most value lands</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Assisted</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Machines do steps, people run the line<em>where most teams are</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Foundations</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Specifications<em>what good looks like</em></span><span class="hl">Automatic checks<em>proof it is good</em></span><span>Recovery<em>fix or roll back fast</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Automation is a spectrum. Every level rests on the same foundations.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>And to back offices</h2>
<p>The same pattern is arriving in knowledge work: claims processing, reconciliation, document review, customer service. AI agents read inputs, apply rules and draft outputs, and people review exceptions. The site's case studies show where this works and where it should stop, from <a href="/case-studies/uk-housing-repairs-ai-agents-adm/">repairs triage at a housing association</a> to <a href="/case-studies/saudi-industrial-ai-agents-maintenance/">maintenance planning at an industrial operator</a>. In both, the agents do the reading and drafting, and people keep the decisions that affect customers or physical assets.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What has to be true before you turn the lights off</h2>
<p>Whether the line makes phones, software or insurance decisions, the preconditions look the same.</p>
<p><strong>1. You can say precisely what "good" is.</strong> A machine can only hit a target it can measure. In software, that means executable specifications and tests that the agents cannot see or edit, so they cannot game them. In back offices, it means written rules and examples of correct outcomes.</p>
<p><strong>2. You can check every output automatically.</strong> If quality depends on a person looking, the line is not dark; it has a hidden bottleneck. Invest in verification before generation. See <a href="/insights/evaluating-ai-agents-reliability/">evaluating AI agents</a>.</p>
<p><strong>3. Failures are contained and cheap to undo.</strong> Factories isolate a faulty station and replace a part without stopping the line. Software platforms do the same: detect the failed unit, replace it and keep everything else running, as the Kubernetes community describes for large distributed jobs<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/dark-factories-automating-work/replace-failed-unit.webp" alt="Diagram of 512 pods where pod 4 has failed and is replaced by a new pod 4 prime, while all the other pods are restarted in place and reused" width="1440" height="340" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>Replace the failed unit, keep the rest running: the property any unattended line needs. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/07/03/navigating-failures-in-pods-with-devices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Sergey Kanzhelev and Mrunal Patel</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p><strong>4. Someone can stop the line.</strong> Every serious factory has a stop button and someone accountable for pressing it. For agents, that means identity per agent, limits on what each can do, and a way to stop one instantly; see the <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-04/">4 November tech news</a> on agent kill switches and <a href="/insights/ai-agent-sandboxing-lessons-anthropic-s1/">agent sandboxing</a>.</p>
<p><strong>5. People still understand the system.</strong> A plant whose engineers no longer understand its machines cannot improve it or recover from novel failures. A codebase nobody reads builds the same "comprehension debt". Keep people reading the parts that matter most, and design for explainability.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Dark factories are not built by removing people. They are built by making quality measurable, failure recoverable and control explicit, and then removing people from the parts where those three are true.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>A sensible path</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Step 1</small><b>Measure</b><ul><li>Define good outcomes</li><li>Build automatic checks</li><li>Baseline today's quality</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Step 2</small><b>Assist</b><ul><li>Automate well-specified steps</li><li>People review every output</li><li>Track where reviews change nothing</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Step 3</small><b>Exception-only</b><ul><li>Remove review where checks are reliable</li><li>People handle exceptions</li><li>Stop button and limits</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Step 4</small><b>Selective dark</b><ul><li>Fully unattended for narrow, reversible work</li><li>Regular audits</li><li>Keep people who understand it</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Move up one step at a time, and only where the evidence says it is safe.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What it means for teams and hiring</h2>
<p>In factories and software alike, the work shifts rather than disappears. Fewer people do the repetitive middle; more of the value sits in specifying what good looks like, building the checks, designing for recovery and handling the exceptions machines cannot. Those roles need deeper understanding of the system, not less. Teams that cut the people who understand the system to fund automation tend to find out why they needed them during the first serious incident. See <a href="/insights/team-topologies-for-ai-teams/">Team Topologies for AI teams</a> and <a href="/insights/ai-coding-policy-engineering-teams/">AI coding policy for engineering teams</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>CTO take</h2>
<p>The dark factory is a useful destination to think about and a poor target to chase. The organisations getting real value, in manufacturing, software and back offices, are those that invest first in measurable quality, automatic checks, contained failure and clear control, and then automate as far as the evidence allows. That is a playbook any company can start on this quarter, whatever its sector or region. If you are deciding how far to automate, start with <a href="/insights/fractional-cto-first-90-days-playbook/">a fractional CTO's first 90 days</a> or <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I can help</h2>
<p>I help teams decide where automation and AI agents can safely run unattended, and build the specifications, checks and controls that make it possible. I am based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. See <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architecture</a> or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
